#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125292} :activity/id #uuid "b218a927-38fe-5672-a4d0-f4c57476e22f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049511 :topic/id #uuid "fabe76f4-9f30-5b59-b906-f5fa71cfb89f" :topic/math-academy-id 1940} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125292 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125733 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the equation of the **tangent line** to the ellipse \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{12} + \\dfrac{y^2}{4} =1\n$$\n\n at the point $P(3,1).$\n\nTo solve this problem, we could find the slope of the line using techniques from single-variable calculus. However, we can also solve the problem by computing the gradient of a two-variable function.\n\nLet's start by defining \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2}{12} + \\dfrac {y^2}{4}.\n$$\n\nThen, the given ellipse is the level curve  $f(x,y)=1.$\n\nNow, we know that $\\nabla \\! f(3,1)$ is a vector that's normal to the level curve $f(x,y)= 1$ at $P(3,1).$ So, it's also normal to the tangent line to our ellipse at $P.$\n\n![](images/3d/3dc75e8fd4083af1bf90f46b2e875d5d552bffdc5c1cfaeabebef70f79f00828.png)\n\nSuppose that $(x,y)$ is any point on that tangent line. Then $\\mathbf v=\\langle x - 3, y-1 \\rangle$ is a vector that's parallel to the tangent line. So $\\mathbf v$ must be also orthogonal to $\\nabla f(3,1),$ and since the dot product of two orthogonal vectors is zero, we have that\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(3,1)\\,\\cdot \\,\\langle x - 3, y-1 \\rangle = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, computing $\\nabla\\! f$ and evaluating it at the point $P(3,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{x}{6},\\frac{y}{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,f(3,1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the equation of our tangent, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(3,1)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(x-3)+\\frac{1}{2}(y-1) & =0 \\\\ (x-3)+(y-1) & =0 \\\\ x+y-4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7a9dede8-0672-56eb-9084-cf581e3fb05f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2496 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7cc9655e-83bb-5f85-8c20-add1d27fb5e0" :step/math-academy-id 11184 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125464}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125293 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124454 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124258 :question/id #uuid "a01560be-9023-593a-87d3-7412f3b208a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3362"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ae7e9c8-60e9-5f5e-afbf-1de6bb01bf07" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050027}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459277}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Line to a Level Curve"} :step/id #uuid "204027bd-d606-5131-82eb-717c2823cc14" :step/math-academy-id 11185 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125294}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125294 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125734 :tutorial/content "Given a level curve $f(x,y) = f(x_0,y_0),$ a **tangent vector** is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(x_0,y_0) = f_y(x_0,y_0) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(x_0,y_0) \\ \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\n \n\nTo understand why the above vector is tangent to the level curve, first recall that the gradient vector $\\nabla f(x_0,y_0)$ is normal to the level curve. Now, by computing the dot product, we see that the vector $\\mathbf t$ is orthogonal to the gradient vector $\\nabla\\! f{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f\\cdot t & =({f}_{x}\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}\\text{ }j)\\cdot ({f}_{y}\\text{ }i-{f}_{x}j) \\\\  & ={f}_{x}{f}_{y}-{f}_{y}{f}_{x} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf t$ is tangent to the level curve." :tutorial/id #uuid "6ef07808-783a-543e-9217-5d3d0a9ae13a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2497 :tutorial/title "The Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"} :step/id #uuid "6501f944-f162-5a6e-9f8e-4ccc2cd2ce41" :step/math-academy-id 11188 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125295}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125295 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124455 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124259 :question/id #uuid "f6400ab7-3684-5f0d-b8f0-1c85b0b322ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-3363"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339122} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499554}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"} :step/id #uuid "1ce011ea-75f6-5023-9ba5-8deb530c69ba" :step/math-academy-id 11189 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125296}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125296 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124456 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124260 :question/id #uuid "c4ca2e8a-5a5b-54c6-983d-2f5e821084ee" :question/math-academy-id "e-3364"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84245283-3cf9-5889-8c42-896a0c8af291" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500338}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points on a Curve at which the Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to a Given Vector"} :step/id #uuid "6e185929-7cee-53dd-9616-e86a123783e5" :step/math-academy-id 11192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125464 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125807 :tutorial/content "Now, let's state the previous result more generally.\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be a function that's continuously differentiable at the point $P(x_0,y_0).$   If  $\\nabla f(x_0, y_0)$ is not the zero vector, then it is orthogonal to the tangent line to the level curve \n\n$$\nf(x,y)= f(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nat the point $P.$ The equation of the tangent can then be written as\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\! f (x_0,y_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0,y-y_0\\rangle  = 0.\n$$\n\nWriting this explicitly, we get\n\n$$\nf_x(x_0,y_0)(x-x_0) + f_y(x_0,y_0)(y-y_0)   = 0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e9c2ee0b-08cf-5f42-bf09-f80dcd82051d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3866 :tutorial/title "Tangent Line to a Level Curve"} :step/id #uuid "2e3c389c-2b5c-55ca-92a8-e52fcbcf9f84" :step/math-academy-id 22698 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125293}}] :activity/title "Tangent Lines to Level Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124732}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482621} :activity/id #uuid "12b9dfa6-9b29-5bbc-991a-88ee2275afa9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049511 :topic/id #uuid "fabe76f4-9f30-5b59-b906-f5fa71cfb89f" :topic/math-academy-id 1940} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482619 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482608 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "644861a7-1cbc-5867-8185-defe9888db93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482005} {:answer/id #uuid "73996ca2-8d53-576d-9599-e7d064a54b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482042} {:answer/id #uuid "8054282c-e26d-5511-a066-a463f3020bfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482072} {:answer/id #uuid "b5877c60-a794-5905-b476-5b03a5663061" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482203} {:answer/id #uuid "f6a04f0f-fb4c-5c47-a171-0ccaf2eedb86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482358}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482072} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ad3bafa-b0de-589d-9a11-64d4563a5603" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eaaccc28-17cb-56b1-b96e-6187ebe91480" :question/math-academy-id "q-130203" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${y}^{2}=1-{x}^{2}+2y.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =1-{x}^{2}+2y \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,2)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(-1,2)={f}_{y}(-1,2)i-{f}_{x}(-1,2)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y)j \\\\  & =(2y-2)i-2x\\,j \\\\ t(-1,2) & =(2(2)-2)i-2(-1)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $2\\,i+2\\,j.$"} :step/id #uuid "02f310c0-3a63-5ca4-a009-b9bb2d7c2097" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482651}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482621 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323918 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18cbb66c-c87e-532e-b215-79cebdd50220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2e-8)\\,i+(\\frac{e}{2}-8)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321343} {:answer/id #uuid "25737ab8-5934-517a-82e3-355f226249ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1-4e)\\,i+(\\frac{2}{e}-8)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321438} {:answer/id #uuid "569fd915-b891-5273-a763-b23c030c775d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2-8e)\\,i+(2-8e)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321831} {:answer/id #uuid "91bfacf0-e81a-591f-b5c7-eb843413fffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{e}-8)i+(8e-2)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322304} {:answer/id #uuid "e6c24a19-a0b8-5781-9f03-f3684c7d7f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(8-2e)\\,i+(\\frac{8}{e}-2)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322954}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322304} :answer-field/id #uuid "2328f82b-ecf4-5e93-897c-028b4fd16484" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "12b276e2-b703-55f7-94b8-069326b3f510" :question/math-academy-id "q-37516" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=8xy+2-8e.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,e).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =8xy+2-8e \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy & =2-8e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=2-8e.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,e)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,e)={f}_{y}(1,e)\\,i-{f}_{x}(1,e)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2x}{y}-8x)i-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8y)\\,j \\\\ t(1,e) & =(\\frac{2(1)}{e}-8(1))i-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-8(e))\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{e}-8)i+(8e-2)j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $(\\frac{2}{e}-8)i+(8e-2)\\,j.$"} :step/id #uuid "05e7d7d3-d815-5b26-b630-27644d17a707" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482641}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403571 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3b898a6-e9f5-51f8-8993-b6f0322af5e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(3,2)$ and $(-1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403573} {:answer/id #uuid "3acac861-9675-5531-8e0f-29301bd84beb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403574} {:answer/id #uuid "879099ba-26c3-5eca-b156-1546984f09e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ and $(-1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403575} {:answer/id #uuid "56e7a278-62d5-5448-adb4-7672d93764ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,2)$ and $(1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403576} {:answer/id #uuid "c66b876c-2af7-5756-8122-0570c4918dbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,3)$ and $(3,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403577}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403575} :answer-field/id #uuid "22c59322-d13f-5287-a0fa-c2f86091a95e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "49dc4b38-854b-53b5-ba44-1bf47c27ef1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-72412" :question/problem "At which points on the curve ${y}^{2}-4xy=-5{x}^{2}+1$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $i?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4xy & =-5{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2})i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2})j \\\\  & =2(y-2x)\\,i-(10x-4y)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i$, we must have $t\\cdot i=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ (2(y-2x)\\,i-(10x-4y)\\,j)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ 2(y-2x) & =0 \\\\ y & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=2x$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (2x)^{2}-4x(2x)+5{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-8{x}^{2}+5{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $x=1$, we get $y=2$ and the corresponding point is $(1,2).$\n\n- If $x=-1$, we get $y=-2$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,-2).$"} :step/id #uuid "6e92b42c-037c-5e2c-9313-5354ee48f332" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482619}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482641 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c870de58-6cf8-5f55-bffc-f699bc46f20d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439921} {:answer/id #uuid "4e20dcfd-74b5-58d8-ade3-1b14c1ebc0f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439922}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439922} :answer-field/id #uuid "565b9b4c-04a0-5048-a56f-8d5e3070a8f0" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 175921864