db/id #edb/ref 17592186439930}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439929} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e3dbaf6-9358-5c2a-9801-aef6c67ddc06" :answer-field/key "V" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440322} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "065e1081-b5f1-5f33-a472-b91a349b1ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439931} {:answer/id #uuid "4bed7d93-95ff-5f0a-9e8a-462b709673d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439932}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439932} :answer-field/id #uuid "a537d884-e552-57b7-b8f8-e2de9abb83a0" :answer-field/key "VI" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440323}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9c0801c9-8ff6-5c1f-a200-5a0877707694" :question/math-academy-id "q-72410" :question/problem "At which points on the curve ${y}^{2}+2xy={x}^{2}+8$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j?$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $(-2,-2)$\n- **II.** $(0,2)$\n- **III.** $(1,2)$\n- **IV.** $(2,0)$\n- **V.** $(2,1)$\n- **VI.** $(2,2)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2xy & ={x}^{2}+8 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=8.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2})i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2})j \\\\  & =2(y+x)\\,i-2(y-x)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ (2(y+x)\\,i-2(y-x)\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ -2(y-x) & =0 \\\\ y & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ (x)^{2}+2x(x)-{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $x=2$, we get $y=2$ and the corresponding point is $(2,2).$\n\n- If $x=-2$, we get $y=-2$ and the corresponding point is $(-2,-2).$"} :step/id #uuid "8010488e-cbf0-5349-af76-b70ec53c1a3b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482636}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482651 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373757 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afbb76f8-a135-56ab-98ff-95aa371de6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439519} {:answer/id #uuid "8c88915e-5b21-5b50-b76c-b452eb74095d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439520}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439519} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb7991eb-11ab-5be8-9cff-8cb4c831a986" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440117} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bee1c3fd-7f28-5bd0-8439-264f31d9f15a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439521} {:answer/id #uuid "aba6457d-6ee5-5643-8c8f-eae80358d7d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439522}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439522} :answer-field/id #uuid "f05aaa51-707b-51f8-bed1-c062eb9077c6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440118} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "049822ce-ef15-5b5d-9864-ade62a37ec43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439523} {:answer/id #uuid "60358451-984b-5b61-929f-f92074afc976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439524}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439524} :answer-field/id #uuid "24967753-af42-5895-a050-8e8c6b225ad6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440119} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a274467-574a-5539-983e-4d583bc9ec92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439525} {:answer/id #uuid "65a64ec9-6cdd-507e-9bfb-590aab6a8b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439526}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439525} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed98d8f5-235d-543c-a23d-ff763ed379a9" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3d9c5851-2645-50db-8b4e-b731824fd7d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-130225" :question/problem "At which points on the curve $2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}=-y$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j?$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $(-1,1)$\n- **II.** $(0,0)$\n- **III.** $(0,\\frac{1}{3})$\n- **IV.** $(1,1)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2} & =-y \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+y)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+y)\\,j \\\\  & =(-6y+1)\\,i-4x\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j,$ we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ ((-6y+1)\\,i-4x\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ -4x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2} & =-y \\\\ 2({0}^{2})-3{y}^{2} & =-y \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-y & =0 \\\\ y(3y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding points are $(0,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{1}{3}).$"} :step/id #uuid "d3f5afa9-3e2d-58c5-8c55-a63599bc185e"}] :activity/title "Tangent Lines to Level Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481766}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499569} :activity/id #uuid "5ef3df9a-43f6-544e-9018-3bb84592edfe" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049511 :topic/id #uuid "fabe76f4-9f30-5b59-b906-f5fa71cfb89f" :topic/math-academy-id 1940} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499569 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124970 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cece21e-845c-5406-87a0-7fadb456d6b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439641} {:answer/id #uuid "de5a9a5b-9a4a-5dff-ae52-46bed3fefdc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439642}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439642} :answer-field/id #uuid "96d13053-52c8-5327-9cdc-96cbda5b9553" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440178} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "035c9f0e-cadd-5cfc-8ae0-b3b87f593d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439643} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d5e2b8-8c44-503b-8c27-5c384d57c17e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439644}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439643} :answer-field/id #uuid "64fa11ce-20a4-54cb-88de-22d21465fcd6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440179} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3046be51-904e-5bd4-a31d-cda140ffe757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439645} {:answer/id #uuid "04e55ddc-c7d8-538f-b3d7-db172d2edb8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439646}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439645} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ebe724a-21ce-5911-9f0e-611acee15d50" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440180} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "065c6ba1-11bd-5ba4-866d-75daa017cda5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439647} {:answer/id #uuid "d416187c-d1f3-5830-8186-2665514f8dbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439648}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439647} :answer-field/id #uuid "08a8c792-30e0-5fbb-bbc3-bd9cc974b2e2" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440181} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4ec5074-b1af-55d2-b87e-abf9b7184442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439649} {:answer/id #uuid "223a19d3-0ddf-546c-8353-4b22a48e3261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439650}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439650} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b25df16-15af-5c49-a2f0-8d1d38790392" :answer-field/key "V" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440182}] :question/id #uuid "fa8c1acf-fd5d-5946-ab8b-13077cafbbf0" :question/math-academy-id "q-36332" :question/problem "At which points on the curve $2xy + 3y^{2} = x^{2} + 4$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j$?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $(-1,-1)$\n- **II.** $(-1,0)$\n- **III.** $(0,1)$\n- **IV.** $(1,-1)$\n- **V.** $(1,1)$" :question/worked-solution "Write the curve as F(x,y)=0. A tangent is (F_y,-F_x); its dot product with the given vector is $2 x - 2 y$. Solving this with the curve equation gives $(-1,-1), (1,1)$, and the tangent is nonzero at each point."} :step/id #uuid "05a2099a-d88b-5b18-b5ca-d033c5db5614" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499600}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499577 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499527 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b1f9282-40f6-534c-9cb9-df688ee64de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-2,-1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498822} {:answer/id #uuid "9c2fce83-b1b2-5397-8cab-576be2461576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499059} {:answer/id #uuid "a70e370b-8012-5ba7-8c81-0ea9db91d6f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,-1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499097} {:answer/id #uuid "d553a226-27d4-508f-a4c8-4c5c6f0cd0cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-2,-1)$  and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499207} {:answer/id #uuid "e67fa0c9-ee34-5eea-a810-dee87b7233d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,-2)$  and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499253}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498822} :answer-field/id #uuid "f38fea1a-0a62-5cc4-9aaa-d50dbb5879df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b5a12ff0-24e0-5963-9f16-1e9a01270fba" :question/math-academy-id "q-72439" :question/problem "At which points on the curve ${y}^{2}-xy=x+1$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $i?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-xy & =x+1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-xy-x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-xy-x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-xy-x)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-xy-x)j \\\\  & =(2y-x)\\,i+(y+1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i$, we must have $t\\cdot i=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ ((2y-x)\\,i+(y+1)\\,j)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ 2y-x & =0 \\\\ x & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2y$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-(2y)y-(2y) & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-2y-1 & =0 \\\\ -{y}^{2}-2y-1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2y+1 & =0 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $y=-1$, we get $x=-2$ and the corresponding point is $(-2,-1).$"} :step/id #uuid "2f4293a9-a10e-5b28-82a5-1a8b2fd293b4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499584}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499584 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363031 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29f3795a-9c5d-564b-99c5-8234a8f8adae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363033} {:answer/id #uuid "17117ea5-c48d-5a75-87dd-44fd4b306516" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363034} {:answer/id #uuid "a1a1d040-84f6-5093-b8b7-a02c512a3ef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363035} {:answer/id #uuid "afb47ff5-03cb-5158-9e15-6ff6eeca5436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363036} {:answer/id #uuid "0c788786-b66b-5944-b4ad-21c56064f8a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i+3\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363037}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363037} :answer-field/id #uuid "d07c4d10-4f15-5f81-9aca-9c05aa1a4821" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "55fb49e6-3301-5747-997a-6aea4f919a61" :question/math-academy-id "q-130201" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $xy=3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy & =3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{3} \\\\ xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=3.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,-1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(-1,-1)={f}_{y}(-1,-1)i-{f}_{x}(-1,-1)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3})i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3})j \\\\  & =(x+3{y}^{2})i-(y+2x)j \\\\ t(-1,-1) & =(-1+3(-1)^{2})i-(-1+2(-1))j \\\\  & =2\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $2\\,i+3\\,j.$"} :step/id #uuid "4cfb1824-7d95-5b0c-8d42-9c86792c679d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499600 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499529 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39ba8864-9639-569c-861f-6e638330546b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498857} {:answer/id #uuid "7b5ff22c-2612-54bb-bf13-52b4667b899d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499006} {:answer/id #uuid "9d918342-1efd-5f5f-a70c-62d3002a283b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499065} {:answer/id #uuid "b5fcce98-7907-5c3f-8f29-c741eb4b7cca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499135} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1550ba-8b0e-51be-878a-b2b84082b1f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499172}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499172} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7015fd4-ab37-5009-8ea6-02df343db6e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b96af04a-99a9-59d6-b4b4-fac54542ff08" :question/math-academy-id "q-65205" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x{e}^{y}-1=xy.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}-1 & =xy \\\\ x{e}^{y}-xy & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}-xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,0)={f}_{y}(1,0)i-{f}_{x}(1,0)j\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}-xy)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}-xy)j \\\\  & =(x{e}^{y}-x)i-({e}^{y}-y)j \\\\ t(1,0) & =((1){e}^{0}-(1))i-({e}^{0}-(0))j \\\\  & =-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-j.$"} :step/id #uuid "8b2ff19d-a282-5f52-b7ed-a3b65431ddba" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499624}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499554 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e1052d2-3a28-5ca0-9e37-9f2ccf48871c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498760} {:answer/id #uuid "0f687a71-756e-59f6-bc52-a42d2a1ff16b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i-3\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498761} {:answer/id #uuid "1ac23b03-c1ad-5ab7-be63-a35fc34705f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i-3\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498787} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf883f8-d823-5d14-9a1b-8d3c64093c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\,i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498942} {:answer/id #uuid "fac357f6-d241-5f53-aa60-7685a0cfadeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499292}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498760} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f7da737-6ab6-5dc3-a356-5e38af59041a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ea587ab0-d077-5468-b262-1697c88c7094" :question/math-academy-id "q-72389" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2{e}^{xy}=x+y+1.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{xy} & =x+y+1 \\\\ 2{e}^{xy}-x-y-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2{e}^{xy}-x-y-1$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(0,1)={f}_{y}(0,1)\\,i-{f}_{x}(0,1)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{e}^{xy}-x-y-1)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{e}^{xy}-x-y-1)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x{e}^{xy}-1)\\,i-(2y{e}^{xy}-1)\\,j \\\\ t(0,1) & =(2(0){e}^{(0)(1)}-1)\\,i-(2(1){e}^{(0)(1)}-1)\\,j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-i-j.$"} :step/id #uuid "dcad53eb-6ded-5184-a27b-881f5dbfc54b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499577}}] :activity/title "Tangent Lines to Level Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498722}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500429} :activity/id #uuid "28f0525e-a9ec-575c-a608-1d31d5ce784d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049511 :topic/id #uuid "fabe76f4-9f30-5b59-b906-f5fa71cfb89f" :topic/math-academy-id 1940} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500393 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500338 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b0c0406-ae8d-54c8-b629-8afd582156ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-2,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499678} {:answer/id #uuid "23b36d38-920a-5a19-90af-0d58ea66b6c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499733} {:answer/id #uuid "4e379d7d-a44b-5b19-ae49-edc70a8dad28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-\\sqrt[3]{4},\\sqrt[3]{4})$ and $(\\sqrt[3]{4},-\\sqrt[3]{4})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499802} {:answer/id #uuid "930ec2dc-3268-5e8c-bb20-3557f9ac1938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-\\sqrt[3]{4},\\sqrt[3]{4})$ and $(-2,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499942} {:answer/id #uuid "fd6ba7c0-fd84-5eed-9805-7ee46c6511bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-\\sqrt[3]{4},\\sqrt[3]{4})$ and $(2,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500174}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499678} :answer-field/id #uuid "956e93fd-6df8-5b5f-8f5c-3b6f83220074" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a5f3b132-60d1-51a8-b2e5-d8b20ae5add9" :question/math-academy-id "q-65209" :question/problem "At which points on the curve $3y{x}^{2}=48+3x{y}^{2}$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $i-j?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y{x}^{2} & =48+3x{y}^{2} \\\\ 3y{x}^{2}-3x{y}^{2} & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=3y{x}^{2}-3x{y}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=48.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3y{x}^{2}-3x{y}^{2})i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3y{x}^{2}-3x{y}^{2})j \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}-6xy)i-(6xy-3{y}^{2})j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i$, we must have $t\\cdot (i-j)=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot (i-j) & =0 \\\\ ((3{x}^{2}-6xy)i-(6xy-3{y}^{2})j)\\cdot (i-j) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-6xy+6xy-3{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-y)(x+y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=y$ or $x=-y.$ Substituting $x=y$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y{x}^{2} & =48+3x{y}^{2} \\\\ 3y{y}^{2} & =48+3y{y}^{2} \\\\ 3{y}^{3} & =48+3{y}^{3} \\\\ 0 & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we get a false statement, we conclude that does not exist points of the form $(x,x)$ on the curve. Substituting $x=-y$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y{x}^{2} & =48+3x{y}^{2} \\\\ 3y(-y)^{2} & =48+3(-y){y}^{2} \\\\ 3{y}^{3} & =48-3{y}^{3} \\\\ 6{y}^{3} & =48 \\\\ {y}^{3} & =8 \\\\ y & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $y=2$, we get $x=-2$ and the corresponding point is $(-2,2).$"} :step/id #uuid "2240391e-6239-5c26-8753-fbe476494f29"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500429 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125191 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "323ab6d8-7707-5cf4-a618-ba17b3923dd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364297} {:answer/id #uuid "043f0c6b-beee-5929-91a6-329b0aba6cab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364298} {:answer/id #uuid "6c7f3a73-6bb3-57e1-b890-8a39b32ac5a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364299} {:answer/id #uuid "b9bd5e2f-eea5-58c3-8f18-2c41ef884348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364300} {:answer/id #uuid "cb787b13-c2e5-5275-b510-4a2ee9675a16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364301}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364297} :answer-field/id #uuid "4633732e-7712-5dd5-9f2e-d57d7262b765" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ad74cdc7-0885-5bf3-989d-0d3835ead91a" :question/math-academy-id "q-65204" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})={f}_{y}(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})i-{f}_{x}(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))\\,j \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))i-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))\\,j \\\\ t(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-0\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}))i-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}))j \\\\  & =-i+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-i+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j.$"} :step/id #uuid "9e08735b-2a3b-57c7-99b1-ddcbb644aa6b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500448}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500448 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125192 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce07dfdf-2d2a-50b4-b231-394b5d5d097f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439857} {:answer/id #uuid "85dfbd24-c291-5c08-b3d8-efa72d2bebc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439858}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439857} :answer-field/id #uuid "76b7e8b2-e034-52dd-9da2-5852ff35bd1c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440286} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75afdcde-1b54-58d6-b950-1389a729ce22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439859} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc181f6-b619-50be-b805-c3f50013f219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439860}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439860} :answer-field/id #uuid "32972e40-590f-5893-946b-6bd1c90198c0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440287} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38c129c4-6cdd-5109-a4f7-14ddc43a724a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439861} {:answer/id #uuid "3e06be53-d9b1-54cd-bff2-19c4f3681747" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439862}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439862} :answer-field/id #uuid "30e09a55-e1b1-529e-9575-29c8323f08a4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440288} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5278cce0-690a-57d3-9f99-59786668dd25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439863} {:answer/id #uuid "c194ddff-249d-568f-bbed-b5a98a9cbf2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439864}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439864} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f6cf21c-f929-519d-97e8-9c947398a3c9" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440289} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd09c21d-1862-5911-bc93-7b66b39ffdd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439865} {:answer/id #uuid "800ae177-fe54-5efa-bdce-f8e4327de277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439866}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439865} :answer-field/id #uuid "50868321-e216-553a-b3ca-72efe4f524e3" :answer-field/key "V" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440290}] :question/id #uuid "142e660e-5b1f-5e61-a343-ee1945f69615" :question/math-academy-id "q-65208" :question/problem "At which points on the curve $9x^{2}y = 3y^{3} - 6x$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $i$?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $(-1,-1)$\n- **II.** $(-1,1)$\n- **III.** $(0,0)$\n- **IV.** $(1,-1)$\n- **V.** $(1,1)$" :question/worked-solution "Write the curve as F(x,y)=0. A tangent is (F_y,-F_x); its dot product with the given vector is $9 x^{2} - 9 y^{2}$. Solving this with the curve equation gives $(-1,1), (0,0), (1,-1)$, and the tangent is nonzero at each point."} :step/id #uuid "e52e5366-4dc8-5cb0-a9bb-eeeb3bfd45c4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500393}}] :activity/title "Tangent Lines to Level Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499643}]}
